{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":783643,"num_turns":33,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8744ca65-6c22-4d91-b1e3-84ad79c48923","total_cost_usd":4.749095000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":8737,"cache_creation_input_tokens":138513,"cache_read_input_tokens":3686458,"output_tokens":57751,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":138513,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6312,"cache_read_input_tokens":158409,"cache_creation_input_tokens":268,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":268},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":33176,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.033276,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":8737,"outputTokens":57751,"cacheReadInputTokens":3686458,"cacheCreationInputTokens":138513,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":4.7158190000000015,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"economy-balancer\",\n  \"flight\": {\n    \"dry_mass_kg\": 80,\n    \"fuel_capacity_kg\": 70,\n    \"thrust_n\": 7470,\n    \"fuel_burn_kgs\": 13.9,\n    \"rcs_thrust_n\": 375,\n    \"rcs_fuel_burn_kgs\": 0.7,\n    \"rotation_rate_degs\": 90\n  },\n  \"cargo\": {\n    \"base_slots\": 6,\n    \"compactor_tier\": 1\n  },\n  \"reentry\": {\n    \"heat_capacity\": 100,\n    \"heat_dissipation_s\": 5,\n    \"cargo_damage_interval_s\": 3,\n    \"unstaged_heat_multiplier\": 3.2,\n    \"off_retrograde_penalty\": 1\n  },\n  \"ablation\": {\n    \"fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band\": { \"suborbital\": 2.0, \"low\": 2.3, \"high\": 2.9 },\n    \"heat_cost_coefficient\": 0.00074,\n    \"heat_cost_exponent\": 2.2,\n    \"heat_index\": { \"suborbital\": 100, \"low\": 103, \"high\": 110 },\n    \"cost_curve\": {\n      \"suborbital\": [20.59, 12.09, 10.97, 11.52, 12.69, 14.16, 15.8, 17.53],\n      \"low\": [22.14, 13.23, 12.21, 12.96, 14.38, 16.11, 18.02, 20.04],\n      \"high\": [25.82, 15.78, 14.83, 15.94, 17.82, 20.07, 22.52, 25.09]\n    },\n    \"plate_capacity_pct\": 100,\n    \"optimal_pass_count\": { \"suborbital\": 3, \"low\": 3, \"high\": 3 }\n  },\n  \"landing\": {\n    \"soft_landing_ms\": 5,\n    \"damage_per_ms_over\": 6,\n    \"no_gear_multiplier\": 2,\n    \"fragile_multiplier\": 2,\n    \"parachute_drag_coefficient\": 1.7,\n    \"parachute_area_m2\": 100,\n    \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms\": 4.6\n  },\n  \"tow_fee\": {\n    \"free_radius_m\": 160,\n    \"max_fee_fraction\": 0.5,\n    \"curve\": \"linear\"\n  },\n  \"eva\": {\n    \"suit_mass_kg\": 20,\n    \"jetpack_thrust_n\": 200,\n    \"jetpack_fuel_kg\": 16,\n    \"jetpack_burn_kgs\": 0.5,\n    \"base_tether_slots\": 2,\n    \"magnet_hold_force_n\": 260,\n    \"magnet_range_screens\": 0.125,\n    \"latch_jerk_coefficient\": 1,\n    \"swing_jerk_coefficient\": 1\n  },\n  \"economy\": {\n    \"launch_cost\": 48,\n    \"replate_cost_per_pct\": 0.6,\n    \"repair_cost_per_pct\": 1.5,\n    \"band_value_multiplier\": { \"suborbital\": 1, \"low\": 2.6, \"high\": 6 },\n    \"size_class_base_value\": { \"small\": 22, \"medium\": 80, \"oversized\": 260 },\n    \"fragile_value_premium\": 4\n  },\n  \"upgrades\": [\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 180, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 92 },\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 520, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 118 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 200, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 9600 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 560, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 12100 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 240, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 9 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 700, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 12 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 260, \"effect\": \"plate_capacity_pct\", \"value\": 140 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 720, \"effect\": \"plate_capacity_pct\", \"value\": 190 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 220, \"effect\": \"parachute_drag_coefficient\", \"value\": 2.55 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 620, \"effect\": \"parachute_drag_coefficient\", \"value\": 4.2 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 190, \"effect\": \"magnet_range_screens\", \"value\": 0.1875 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 540, \"effect\": \"magnet_hold_force_n\", \"value\": 380 }\n  ],\n  \"balance_notes\": [\n    \"FINDING 1, full_hold_doubles_mass (GDD 2.3.5). dry_mass_kg moves 45 -> 80, chosen so that every reading of 'a full hold' lands inside the 25% tolerance instead of only the suborbital-only one. Spawn-weighted over the hand-tetherable shipping-slice population with the revised catalog: crushing every non-fragile piece at tier 1 gives 3034 kg / 195 slots = 15.56 kg/slot, so 6 slots = 93.4 kg and all-up mass is 173.4 kg, a ratio of 2.17x. Crushing only smalls at tier 1 (the stricter reading of GDD 2.3.4) gives 3034/267 = 11.36 kg/slot, 68.2 kg, 1.85x. The two single-band extremes bracket it: suborbital-only 8.77-10.44 kg/slot = 1.66-1.78x, low-only 12.92-19.43 kg/slot = 1.97-2.46x. Worst case across all four readings is -17% and +23% of doubling, both inside 25%, where the previous 45 kg ship flew at 3.01x. 80 kg is not a suborbital-only anchor; it is the geometric middle of the two crush rules over the whole slice mix.\",\n    \"FINDING 3, parachute_descent_under_soft_line (GDD 2.3.2). The canopy is now stated rather than inferred: landing.parachute_area_m2 = 100 with parachute_drag_coefficient 1.7 gives Cd*A = 170 m2. Terminal velocity v = sqrt(2*m*g/(rho0*Cd*A)) at the real 6-slot hold, m = 80 + 93.4 = 173.4 kg, g = 30, rho0 = 3: sqrt(10404/1530) = 2.61^0.5 scaled = 4.60 m/s, and the integrated descent touches down at 4.58 m/s empty-of-error against the 5.0 line. Empty is 3.09 m/s and half hold 3.90 m/s, so the sqrt-of-mass law is visible across the load range and the 4.6 figure is derived at the actual full hold, not at twice dry mass. The Parachute tiers re-space above it (2.55, 4.2) and are what make Storage buyable: a 12-slot hold is 186.7 kg of cargo, which lands at 5.68 m/s on the base canopy (hard) and 4.64 m/s on Parachute tier 1 - the paired purchase GDD 2.3.6 asks for.\",\n    \"FINDING 2, ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes (GDD 2.3.1) - the curve is fixed and the model is now anchored to measurement, but I could not make the FLOWN optimum move, and I want to be exact about why rather than claim a fix. heat_index is no longer asserted: 100 / 103 / 110 are the measured peak heats of a committed single-pass descent from each band midpoint at the new mass, normalised so suborbital reads 100, and their 1.10x spread agrees with the Researcher's finding that entry speed only spans 151-164 m/s across the bands. Recomputing cost(band, n) = n*toll + 0.00074*n*(H/n)^2.2: suborbital n=1 = 2.0 + 18.59 = 20.59, n=3 = 6.0 + 4.97 = 10.97, n=4 = 8.0 + 3.52 = 11.52; low n=3 = 6.9 + 5.30 = 12.21; high n=1 = 2.9 + 22.94 = 25.82, n=3 = 8.7 + 6.14 = 14.83, n=4 = 11.6 + 4.35 = 15.94. All three minima sit at index 3 and match optimal_pass_count. The coefficient and tolls are both halved from the last pass (0.00148 -> 0.00074, 4.0/4.6/5.8 -> 2.0/2.3/2.9), which leaves n* = H*(c*(e-1)/T)^(1/e) unchanged because it depends only on c/T, but brings a full-hold descent from the low band down from 108% of plate to 54% - previously the worst legal haul could not come home on a base shield at all.\",\n    \"WHAT I COULD NOT FIX, and it is not the ablation coefficients. The flown cheapest_pass_count stays 1 and no value I own changes that, for a reason that is structural rather than tuned: the heat scale is calibrated so that an empty single-pass descent from the suborbital band reads exactly 100, and that same trajectory is then the measurement the 2-4 pass rule is scored against - so pass count 1 is the reference by construction and every multi-pass descent is measured as hotter. I checked this against the flight model across a 33x mass range (dry 90, 250, 500, 1200, 3000 kg, staged ballistic coefficient 17.7 to 591) and the coolest suborbital multi-pass descent peaked at 104-110 in every single case, never below the 100 reference. The second reason is that the pass counter counts atmospheric entries, so a long shallow decaying entry that never leaves the air counts as one pass - the design's 'single plunge' and the simulator's 'n=1' are not the same trajectory. Both are measurement definitions upstream of me. The auditor's named lever, ballistic coefficient, is real but out of reach: at the baseline's reference_area_m2 3.5 and Cd 1.45 the staged figure is 80/5.075 = 15.8 kg/m2, and reaching the baseline's own 98.5 would need a 500 kg ship, which is 5.4x heavier than the doubling rule permits against this catalog.\",\n    \"CONSISTENT REFERENCE AREA, which is the one number that would reconcile findings 1 and 2 and which I have no field for. The Researcher lists ship dry mass 500 kg and reference_area_m2 3.5 as assumptions, not research outputs, and states that every drag and heat figure scales as Cd*A/m. An 80 kg ship reproduces the baseline's own 98.5 kg/m2 staged ballistic coefficient at reference_area_m2 = 80/(1.45*98.5) = 0.560 m2 - a 0.84 m ship, which is the right size for a hold that carries 93 kg of scrap. Until the baseline publishes 0.56 rather than 3.5, the params and the physics document describe two different ships and the multi-pass ladder cannot exist in either. This is a Researcher change; I have flagged it rather than quietly compensating for it, because compensating would mean moving dry mass to 500 and failing GDD 2.3.5 instead.\",\n    \"LAZY RUN BREAKS EVEN (GDD 2.3.6). launch_cost 48 buys a fresh stage and a full 70 kg tank. The cheapest thing in the sky is a suborbital small: size_class_base_value.small 22 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 22, so three of them are 66 and the launch is 2.2 cheap pieces - inside the 'two to three' the GDD names, with 18 to spare. A genuinely lazy run - six crushed suborbital smalls in a 6-slot hold, 132 - clears the launch and the replating of a 20.6% suborbital plunge (0.6 x 20.6 = 12.4) with 71 left over. Ascent is measured, not assumed: thrust 7470 N against a 150 kg wet ship is a liftoff TWR of 1.66 at g = 30, and the integrated gravity turn reaches the suborbital band with 18.9% of the tank left and the low band with 12.8%, both above the 8% floor the return burn needs. The high band is reachable at 6% margin, which is not enough to come back from - band 3 is a fuel_tank purchase away, exactly as GDD 2.3.7 wants.\",\n    \"NO SHEAR UNDER STEADY TOW (GDD 2.3.4). Astronaut acceleration is jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + towed mass), and per-cable tension is that acceleration times the piece's own mass. The heaviest legal two-piece load in the shipping slice is two satellite_bus_frame at 62 kg: a = 200/(20+124) = 1.389 m/s2 and tension = 62 x 1.389 = 86.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 260, a 3.0x margin. The heaviest in the whole catalog, two rtg_core_shell at 96 kg, is worse-looking and actually safer per cable: a = 200/212 = 0.943 and tension = 90.6 N, 2.9x margin. This is the GDD's counterintuitive rule falling out of the arithmetic rather than being asserted - adding mass lowers acceleration faster than it raises per-piece tension, so a full string is sluggish, not fragile. Shear is left entirely to the jerk terms: at latch_jerk_coefficient 1.0 a 62 kg piece shears at 260/62 = 4.2 m/s of closing speed, which is a careless approach and not a normal one. Empty-handed the astronaut pulls 10.0 m/s2 and a two-medium load drops that to 1.39 - a 7.2x change in handling, which is the greed decision made physical.\",\n    \"TOW FEE (GDD 2.3.3). free_radius_m 160 is one screen at the hand magnet's 0.125-screen reach; beyond it the fee is 0.5 * (d - 160) / (2513.3 - 160), reaching exactly max_fee_fraction 0.5 at half the planet's circumference, pi * 800 = 2513.3 m. It is zero inside the pad zone, linear between, clamped at 0.5, and applied to haul value only - it cannot go negative and never touches savings.\",\n    \"WHAT I DID NOT TOUCH, so the next audit is readable. Only four blocks moved from the last pass: flight (mass scale and the thrust/tank that follow from it, engine exhaust velocity held at 537 m/s), ablation (heat_index re-anchored to measurement, coefficient and tolls halved together), landing (canopy published, base drag coefficient 1.75 -> 1.7), and the fuel_tank and thruster upgrade values that had to follow the new base. cargo, reentry, tow_fee, eva and economy are byte-identical to the audited pass, and every quantity the GDD fixes in prose is unchanged: base_slots 6, base_tether_slots 2, soft_landing_ms 5.0, magnet_range_screens 0.125, compactor_tier 1, no_gear_multiplier 2, fragile_multiplier 2, cargo_damage_interval_s 3, heat_dissipation_s 5, max_fee_fraction 0.5, twelve purchases.\",\n    \"OBSERVATION FOR THE HUMAN, not a rule failure. A shallow unstaged braking pass at 65 m - the coarse-braking phase GDD 2.3.1 opens the descent with - measures a peak of 687 on the 0-100 bar, so it pegs the heat bar and starts damaging cargo long before the player ever stages. At unstaged_heat_multiplier 1.0 it would still read 215. The multiplier is not what is wrong: a naked hull at Cd 0.45 lingers in the air for tens of seconds and the 5-second heat bar reaches its equilibrium, while the staged shield's committed plunge is over in a few. Either the pre-staging braking phase is not survivable as specified, or heat_dissipation_s is too slow for shallow passes to read as cool - and since the GDD fixes that at 5 seconds, I have left it alone and flagged it rather than tuning the number the rule is measured against.\"\n  ],\n  \"catalog_concerns\": [\n    \"satellite_bus_frame, mass_kg 62: the whole tetherable mass scale, not this piece alone, is what forces the ballistic-coefficient failure. A 6-slot hold of slice junk weighs 68-93 kg, so GDD 2.3.5's doubling rule caps the ship at about 80 kg dry - one low-band medium is then 78% of the entire rocket. To let the ship be the 500 kg the baseline assumes while still doubling under load, the hand-tetherable population would need roughly 5x its current mass (about 83 kg/slot). That is the Debris Designer's call and it fights the 'small is a slot cost, not a mass promise' teaching, so the alternative fix - a reference_area_m2 near 0.56 from the Researcher - is probably the cheaper one; I am raising both so the choice is visible.\",\n    \"tungsten_ballast_slug, mass_kg 55 at size_class small: at 55 kg per crushed slot it is 3.5x the slice's 15.56 kg/slot average, so six of them weigh 330 kg against an 80 kg ship - a 5.1x mass ratio that no parachute tier in the shipping slice can land softly (Parachute tier 2 still touches down at 7.3 m/s). The piece is doing exactly the teaching job its design note claims, but it sits outside the load range the chute is tuned across. Worth confirming that a full slug hold is meant to be unlandable rather than merely expensive.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":10624,"ttft_stream_ms":1623,"time_to_request_ms":381,"type":"result","duration_ms":863431,"uuid":"6ee39ce0-f9dc-48cc-822e-ac79c76d5b56"}
